# BLENDER SOURCES GO TO LFS. Only us_base_v3.blend was tracked, so every other .blend went into
# git history as a raw blob - huey_lz_staging.blend is 51 MB and GitHub warned on the push. A
# .blend is saved dozens of times over its life and git keeps a WHOLE NEW COPY of each save, so
# one untracked staging file quietly writes hundreds of megabytes into history that cannot be
# reclaimed without rewriting it. This is the art storage-bloat law pointed at version control.
#
# FUTURE COMMITS ONLY. Blobs already in history stay there until someone chooses to rewrite it,
# which is destructive and the Summoner's call, not an incidental cleanup.
*.blend filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
*.blend1 filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
